\subsubsection{Oscillatory transport on a nonconvex annulus}
\label{sec:adr-oscillatory}

A manufactured cellular-flow problem combines a narrow-necked domain with
rapidly varying transport and weak, rotated diffusion. It provides a substantially
harder stationary HDG system than the smooth constant-velocity reference while
retaining an exact solution for accuracy checks.

\paragraph{Problem and geometry.}
On the five-lobed annulus
\begin{equation}
 \Omega=\{(r,\theta):\;0.58<r<1+0.35\cos(5\theta)\},
 \qquad -\nabla\!\cdot(K\nabla u)+\nabla\!\cdot(\boldsymbol\beta u)+c u=f,
 \label{eq:adr-stress-domain}
\end{equation}
set exact Dirichlet data on both boundary components and prescribe
\begin{align}
 u_\star(x,y)&=\sin(6\pi x)\sin(5\pi y)
               +0.35\sin(11\pi x)\sin(9\pi y),\label{eq:adr-stress-exact}\\
 \boldsymbol\beta(x,y)&=
 \begin{pmatrix}1+4\sin(7\pi x)\cos(7\pi y)\\
                 \tfrac12-4\cos(7\pi x)\sin(7\pi y)\end{pmatrix},
 \quad c=10^{-2},\label{eq:adr-stress-velocity}\\
 K&=R(\pi/6)\operatorname{diag}(10^{-4},10^{-6})R(\pi/6)^T,
 \label{eq:adr-stress-tensor}
\end{align}
where $R$ is a planar rotation. Since $\nabla\!\cdot\boldsymbol\beta=0$, the
manufactured source is
$f=-K_{11}u_{\star,xx}-2K_{12}u_{\star,xy}-K_{22}u_{\star,yy}
+\boldsymbol\beta\cdot\nabla u_\star+c u_\star$.
The velocity is a drifted version of the sinusoidal cellular-flow family studied
by Haynes and Vanneste;\footnote{P.~H. Haynes and J. Vanneste,
\emph{Dispersion in the large-deviation regime. Part II: Cellular flow at large
P\'eclet number} (2014), \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.6666}.}
the exact solution, drift, tensor, reaction and annulus here are manufactured
extensions. The characteristic ratio $4/(7\pi\lambda_{\min}(K))\simeq1.8\times10^5$
quantifies the weak diffusion. The isotropic weak-diffusion variant keeps the other fields unchanged:
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 -10^{-5}\Delta u+\nabla\cdot(\boldsymbol\beta u)+10^{-2}u&=f,
 &u|_{\partial\Omega}&=u_\star.
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:adr-stress-isotropic}
\end{equation}

The nominal minimum neck width is $0.07$. Affine triangular meshes use a
400-segment outer polygon and a polygonal circular hole, with exact data applied
to the actual discrete edges. At target sizes $0.04$ and $0.02$, there are
6,114 and 22,825 triangles, respectively; at $p=6$ these give 62,475 and
236,502 free trace unknowns. Minimum triangle angles are $26.1^\circ$ and
$30.2^\circ$. The field need not be tangent to the walls: this is a Dirichlet
transport test. Geometry alone need not worsen convergence; published work on
perforated transport problems shows that the boundary condition on holes also
matters.\footnote{C. Le Bris, F. Legoll and S. Madiot,
\emph{Multiscale Finite Element methods for advection-dominated problems in
perforated domains}, \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.09331}.}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{\ADRStressFigurePath/geometry_fields.pdf}
 \caption{Coarse annular mesh, exact oscillatory solution and drifted cellular
 velocity. The central hole and five narrow passages combine geometry and
 coefficient variation without introducing a corner-singular exact solution.}
 \label{fig:adr-stress-fields}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Matched solver protocol.}
One NVIDIA RTX PRO 5000 Blackwell GPU runs serial FP64 solves of the same
unscaled Bernstein trace matrix and right-hand side, with fixed face stabilization
$\tau=1+\max(\boldsymbol\beta\cdot n,0)$, from an explicitly zeroed
initial guess. Acceptance requires solver convergence and an independently
recomputed $\|b-Ax\|_2/\|b\|_2\leq10^{-10}$; the internal target is $10^{-11}$,
the iteration cap is 1,000 and the GMRES restart length is 75.
Block additive Schwarz uses ASM+PP(24) with CGS2 GMRES.
AMGX uses natural $7\times7$ face BSR blocks at $p=6$: either a classical
block-AMG V-cycle with one pre/post DILU sweep, or direct block DILU.
The two AMG rows differ only in their outer Krylov method. These are fixed
configurations transferred from the preceding ADR comparison, using the same
block-AMG hierarchy parameters; they are not an exhaustive tuning
study of this new problem.

Each $p=6$ result is the mean second-solve time over three fresh setups, after
one excluded warmup setup; both solves start from zero. Preconditioner setup and
accuracy checks are excluded from the heatmap timings. The companion CSV
records timing ranges and setup separately. An initial $p=4$ square screening
uses two measured setups. In the original comparison, @@COUNTS@@
The $p$MG--AMG extension attempts both policies on all 17 distinct initial
systems and converges on 13. Standard uses direct transfer to degree zero,
order-two Chebyshev smoothing and one pre/post sweep; robust uses degree
halving, order-four smoothing and two pre/post sweeps. Both precondition the
symmetric part while GMRES solves the full ADR system.

