\end{figure}

\paragraph{Difficulty and solver behavior.}
@@FINDINGS@@
On the fine annulus, block-AMG/FGMRES takes 230 iterations and 1.85~s,
and block-AMG/BiCGSTAB takes 162 iterations and 2.29~s; direct DILU/BiCGSTAB
fails at the cap. Median setup times are 322~ms for ASM and 202~ms for
either AMG outer method, so ASM remains cheaper for a fresh solve here. For $K=10^{-5}I$, ASM+PP takes 300 iterations and 1.51~s,
while all three tested AMGX configurations fail at 1,000 iterations.
The lower iteration count of BiCGSTAB does not imply a cheaper solve.
Both $p$MG--AMG policies also fail on these two fine-annulus stress variants.
ASM+PP therefore retains its advantage after $p$MG--AMG coverage is completed.
On the coarse weak-anisotropic annulus, robust $p$MG--AMG converges but remains
slower than ASM+PP; standard $p$MG--AMG fails. Iteration growth under refinement
remains substantial.

Changing only the exact solution and source leaves the matrix byte-for-byte
unchanged on both the square and coarse annulus; this control takes the same
8 or 9 ASM iterations as the corresponding smooth reference. Oscillatory
forcing alone therefore does not change matrix conditioning. On the coarse
annulus, reproducible lower estimates of $\kappa_1(A)$ rise from
$1.47\times10^4$ (base) to $1.51\times10^5$ (anisotropic) and
$4.14\times10^5$ (low isotropic). Relative Frobenius asymmetry rises from
$0.540$ to $0.942$ and $0.946$. These are diagnostics in the fixed Bernstein
basis, not exact spectral condition numbers or a complete explanation of
preconditioned nonnormal Krylov convergence. The coefficient, diffusion and
reaction controls are retained in the data archive.

\paragraph{Accuracy and cost attribution.}
Fourteen systems pass independent CPU direct-solution comparisons;
the fine annular systems are checked by physical residual and analytic PDE
error. Across the original 390 solves belonging to passing configurations, the largest
physical relative residual is $9.98\times10^{-12}$, and saved trace solutions
agree between solvers within $3.06\times10^{-9}$ relative to ASM.
Table~\ref{tab:adr-stress-accuracy} shows the reconstructed primal errors.
Doubling both quadrature point counts from 14 to 28 on the coarse annulus changes the
primal field by less than $0.033\%$ of its discretization error; the anisotropic
probe also preserves all four solver outcomes and iteration counts.
No formal convergence order is inferred from just these two unstructured,
polygonally approximated domains.

